{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A 26 '\"'^## D(699|537|368)'\"\\\\b' ../../DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba ../../prd/06-views.md | sed -n '114,137p'; sed -n '300,377p' ../../../AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"3960:## D368 · 2026-11-05 · Alex's answers to the seven items left open by the consolidation\n3961-\n3962-- Alex, on the seven items at the head of `docs/DECISIONS.md` (D367): \"1. 这个你记住，我们要做 2. 做 3. 好那就按照后面的那条来 4. 好你来定 5. 你要来帮我看到底做不做\n3963-  6. 你看该怎么处理核实 7. 不，用英文吧。\"; then \"你开始逐条改，不要drift, 记录下来把1-7都清理了我们再commit push,开始快速做\".\n3964-- Decided:\n3965-  1. The asset-thesis rungs stated against guidance, each thesis naming its favourable end (D186), and the rename of `industry_aggregate`\n3966-     to `shared_quantity` (D191 point 4) are built in the next extraction version. The frozen v74i stays until then (D274); the new\n3967-     version is measured against it.\n3968-  2. The three decided rules not yet built are built, each designed and measured as its own slice (AGENTS §2, §7): a company's own\n3969-     quantity is a thesis under that company, private companies included (D187); a later item may add a dependency to an existing thesis\n3970-     (D191 point 2); the matching step chooses a new thesis's dependencies (D51), which the default Jev path does not ask today.\n3971-  3. At the due day pull mode closes the due questions and does nothing else (D356); D248 boundary 2's step that opens the next-deadline\n3972-     successor of an event question falling due unresolved is dropped.\n3973-  4. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation, by D208's design: a seed question's dependency arrow means \"supports\". For filing, the\n3974-     supporting (driver) question is the narrower one, so a statement linked to both goes to it, as `extraction.narrowest_links` does\n3975-     today. For judging, the supported (root) question reads the supporting questions' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged\n3976-     before their roots. Today the judging code reads the stored edge the other way: the driver reads the root and is judged after it\n3977-     (`seeding.seed_views` stores the seed's `depends_on` as the driver's dependency). The fix separates the two uses of the edge; it\n3978-     changes judgements, so it is measured before it enters the default.\n3979-  5. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation: PRD 10 is corrected without changing any rule. §4 names the frozen standard's version 5 and\n3980-     §5 carries a status line (its links-rule version failed in §8 step 5; D361 keeps the fallback sentence).\n3981-  6. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation: the three statements of the frozen extraction standard page that later decisions changed are\n3982-     noted in a dated correction record beside it, `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard-corrections.md`; the page is not edited (D261). Each\n3983-     statement was found verbatim in the page: \"抽取看不到库\" (§4 and §5.3; changed by D251 and D264), \"研究的交付物是论证\" (§7; the\n3984-     argument record set aside on 2026-11-04) and \"只有事实能确定落在哪一档\" (§5 and §7; changed by D260 and D263).\n3985-  7. Pages written for Alex's review are in English, like the rest of the maintained documentation (D163); PRD 10 is translated, his\n3986-     quotes kept in their own language. What is said to him keeps the plain-language rule (AGENTS, Authority and scope).\n--\n6457:## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n6458-\n6459-- **The census** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md`): over the fifteen\n6460-  questions the old system opened in the stored unseeded fresh week, 97 pairs on which a dependency could have been chosen (each\n6461-  new question against the questions it was compared with and its same-article siblings). Claude's own read written first, then\n6462-  two blind readers of different model families (the same mark on 68 of 97; both mark \"the new question would want the earlier\n6463-  one's conclusion\" on 11, three of them strong). One clear case: data-center revenue FY2027 against total revenue FY2027 (the\n6464-  matching engine recorded \"part of\", 1.00): the total's conclusion carried the year's consensus ($406.05 billion from 09-23,\n6465-  about $412 billion from 09-25) and the $108 billion guidance (from 09-26), none of them anywhere in the data-center question's\n6466-  seven requests; its last answer names the gap and adds a step of its own. Negative controls: the Hugging Face commitments\n6467-  question (\"none\" in all three readings); a weakly granted pair whose line would carry a judge's own assumed numbers. The\n6468-  strongest driver relations of the week (Rubin shipping against the revenue questions) run from a later question to an earlier\n6469-  one and cannot be chosen at creation (D191 point 2, decided, unbuilt).\n6470-- **The council's corrections:** the $96.2 billion quarter was already in all seven requests (not a figure the line would\n6471-  supply); the one \"same question\" pair is one unordered pair; the line would not by itself make the answer supported.\n6472-- **Decided, citing D439:** the pair is a positive lead for the decided dependency-selection slice (D368 item 2), not a\n6473-  validation of it. Before the council reviews any six-call send on it: a bounded proof on stored data of what the edge would do\n6474-  to filing and to judging (a statement linked to both questions, the direction, ordering, cycle rejection, what the judge's\n6475-  request would contain, the unrelated pair as a negative case), with no frozen file changed. Stop, and return the conflict to\n6476-  the council as a design question, if the edge makes filing discard a home contrary to the governing rule, creates a cycle,\n6477-  cannot be represented without conflating the two meanings of the edge, or fails to show the intended conclusion.\n6478-\n6479-## D538 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: one dependency edge shows the intended conclusion to the judge and removes seven filing links; the dependency slice's design is opened at $0, beginning with the separation of the edge's two uses (council consensus, reflection round 29)\n6480-\n6481-- **The proof** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/EDGE-PROOF-RESULT.md`, `edge_proof.py`, $0): on scratch\n6482-  copies of the stored unseeded fresh week, the edge \"data-center revenue FY2027 depends on total revenue FY2027\" written by the\n6483-  writer's own steps. Judging, conditional on the rest of the week as stored: thirteen registered expectations hold (the reverse\n--\n10505:## D699 · 2026-11-21 · Alex continues with A: the whole-window measurement of the linking version, designed with the council at $0 first\n10506-\n10507-- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21), replying to the next-step question after D698 (A, recommended: run poc-v119 over 07-01..09-20 once,\n10508-  about $9 as one poc-v118 run cost, its measurement designed with the council at $0 before any send: whether related number questions\n10509-  still contradict each other, which links the matcher chose and whether any is wrong, whether the self-made market reference becomes\n10510-  rarer, whether other questions get worse; then decide whether the new version replaces the product. B: stop, poc-v118 stays): \"continue\".\n10511-- **Decided (Alex):** A. The $0 design and its council come first. The ~$9 was an estimate, not a ceiling: the paid send waits for the\n10512-  council's pass and a ceiling Alex names. poc-v118 stays the product until a later decision.\n10513-- **Next:** the design (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/`), its $0 proofs, council rounds, then the ceiling question to Alex.\n   114\t## Dependencies: reading the arrows\n   115\t\n   116\t<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n   117\t```\n   118\tB depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n   119\t├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)\n   120\t│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   121\t│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)\n   122\t│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n   123\t└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)\n   124\t    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   125\t    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)\n   126\t    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”\n   127\t```\n   128\t\n   129\t- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.\n   130\t- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n   131\t- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.\n   132\t- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Daily filing gives a statement one home: the narrowest open thesis it bears on, as that thesis's own figure, step or estimate or through the demand, supply, competition or pricing behind its answer, plus the same answer's candidates (D207, D259, D264, D265); the separate coverage-start reading was retired (D370). Dependencies are chosen only when B is created; a later item adding a dependency to an existing thesis is decided (D191 point 2) and to be built (D368).\n   133\t- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.\n   134\t- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies through the default matcher; building the choice on the Jev path is decided (D368). The historical seed questions are retired (D370). A historical seed's dependency arrow means \"supports\" (D368, by D208): for filing, the supporting (driver) thesis is the narrower one; for judging, the supported (root) thesis reads the supporting theses' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged before their roots. The judging code reads the stored edge the other way today; the fix is to be built and measured (D368). Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n   135\t- Before adding an edge or following it to a successor, code checks for cycles. Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling. Apparently mutually dependent questions indicate poorly defined theses.\n   136\t<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n   137\t\n  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n  (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子\").\n- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind a metric's largest\n  values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.\n- **Report every number, regressions first.** For future evidence evaluation, combine the existing gold's core and supporting entries as helpful, use its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral (D372). Count one research matter in one article (D250), not raw sentences. Retain the old labels and scores in historical results, and do not silently relabel the frozen gold. One quantity per table column. A comparison of\n  totals uses three draws; a count from one draw is labelled one draw and shows a mechanism, not a difference between arms. Lead with the\n  mechanism and the share of its class it explains, then the fix.\n- **Read what was dropped.** A statement the pipeline drops is thrown away with its reason, never parked on a side list; evaluation\n  reads the dropped statements by hand, classifies them by the mechanism that would have kept them, and fixes the largest class upstream\n  (D209: \"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up\").\n- **Measure end to end before a step's arm, and read the parked designs first.** A step's noise that the next step removes is not the\n  defect, and a step's recall that the next step throws away is. Before any arm on one step: the production replay through the whole path\n  on the gold window, the count at the step the product reads, and the decisions on the topic read for a parked fix (Alex, 2026-10-31:\n  \"make sure we are not going around in circles using wrong ruler and wrong prompts that don't work\"). Before a phase targets one step,\n  the upside of that step done perfectly is measured where the product is judged: the judge is given the gold's own evidence, built by\n  the production code, a few sends (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"我很不开心你的失败，为什么，深度多轮检讨记录，想思路突破\").\n- **Read what was kept against the gold, blind, before the next noise arm.** The not-kept gold entries a version keeps are judged by a\n  reader who wrote neither the gold nor the prompt, mixed with controls it cannot tell apart; where it parts from the gold, the standard\n  is unclear or the gold misreads it, and that goes to Alex before any arm counts it as noise (Alex, 2026-10-31: \"你再次重新检查尺子，看看是不是这些留下的还有歧义；然后再思考怎么降低noise\").\n- Pair every structural check with its semantic one. Run a data rule over its whole input range and on cases it was not tuned on. Weigh\n  a case by what it carries in the product, not by count.\n- A claim about a population (\"the data is clean\") rests on a code sweep of the whole population and an independent read of a fresh\n  random sample, never on the pages the author looked at.\n- Cost is priced from tokens at the published rates (`hivemind.model.PRICES`, cached input at `model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE`), never from a\n  stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.\n\n### 7.3 Iterating\n\n- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n  the next test goes straight ahead, held to the PRD and its quantified goals, with every trace read by you yourself and not only\n  by readers (Alex, 2026-11-10: \"你应该跑council loop 来reflect 你每一轮的思路还有决策，需要你打成共识，记录决策，向前直行测试。不要drift,我们有PRD和量化目标，\n  你自己作为llm 下去看所有trace\").\n- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n  control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the model's own account and the\n  request locate where the decision is made (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你跑这么多次真的有意义吗\"; \"我感觉你在为了跑而跑\"). Several fixes on one\n  base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02: \"快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整\"; D266).\n- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n  rounds of review (Claude's own, Codex's, an independent reader's) on the traces until a round finds nothing serious; never a menu of\n  arms or options, never a conclusion the next probe overturns (a choice that changes what the system does still goes to him with its\n  options and a recommendation, Authority and scope) (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"就测试一种，一次 … 你就没有自己看5轮review，能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议？\").\n  An agreed recommendation is first challenged by an independent reader on the concrete evidence behind it (\"why you give\n  recommendations for this. What did you see exactly?\"). A review round reads a rule as its author meant it; only a send shows how the\n  model applies it: before a registered test, the changed request is sent on the cases that motivated it, a few cents.\n- **A failed arm is diagnosed before the next one.** When an arm fixes its class and regresses another, write the root cause first: the\n  process error (what grew, what the small sample did not guard) and the mechanism, traced to the raw answers and the model's account,\n  never a guess stated as the cause. A small sample guards every class the change can move, not only its own. When a fix keeps moving\n  another class, the call is doing several jobs: move the rule to its owner (a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field)\n  rather than add a sentence (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"那你这些修改都是失败的呀，为什么，根因，你要检讨然后再接着尝试。我觉得你已经陷入deep loop 了\").\n  A failed step of a plan is fixed and re-run at that same step: its failures are the work list, and nothing later in the plan and no\n  substitute plan starts before it passes (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"你为什么要drift? 你现在明明还有这么多的问题要fix,你到底知不知道你要fix什么？\").\n  Before a fix is proposed, write which goal the mechanism still serves and what the product already gives for it; a measurement that\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/JULY-RESULT.md; nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/JULY-RESULT.md; nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The July test of the linking version: PASS by the frozen rule (D694 (c), D696, D697; 2026-11-21; $0.0689)\n     2\t\n     3\tRun: `poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/` (`send-summary.json`: complete; $0.0689 spent of the $0.15 ceiling; 39 provider attempts;\n     4\tnothing refused or overrun). Six sequences from old-2's state at the end of 07-09, judged fresh 07-10..07-12: three under poc-v118\n     5\t(controls), three under poc-v119 (linked). In all three linked sequences the matcher chose and the writer committed \"v9 (Data Center\n     6\tFY2027) reads v14 (total revenue FY2027)\", `first_is_bullish`, at seq 148, and no other link. Every v9 and v14 judgement was a fresh\n     7\tcall. Rule: `reading-rule.md` (frozen before the send, sha256 cc876b60…).\n     8\t\n     9\t## Marks (07-12 answers; harm checks over the window)\n    10\t| Sequence (blind label) | v14 total | v9 Data Center | Claude (`claude-marks.md`, written first) | Blind Codex reader (`codex-blind.md`) |\n    11\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    12\t| linked-1 (K) | ~$410B | ~$378B = v14 x 92.2% | A–D yes | A–D yes |\n    13\t| linked-2 (M) | ~$400B | ~$356B run-rate, checked below v14 | A–D yes (D as a stated bound) | no: D a cross-check only; v14's crossing of $400B unsized |\n    14\t| linked-3 (N) | ~$387B | ~$356–360B = v14 x 92% | A–D yes | A–D yes |\n    15\t| control-1 (Q) | ~$411B | ~$370B: run-rate + unsized ~$14B | A–C yes (uplift not material) | no: the uplift unsized (B) |\n    16\t| control-2 (L) | ~$412B | ~$405B: run-rate + unsized ~$48B | no (B) | no (B) |\n    17\t| control-3 (P) | ~$411B | $390–395B, second-half growth unstated | no (B) | no (B) |\n    18\t\n    19\tLinked meeting A–D: Claude 3 of 3, reader 2 of 3. Controls meeting A–C: Claude 1 of 3, reader 0 of 3. First-attempt refusals: none.\n    20\tGrounding errors as the rule defines them: none (both readers). The reader identified all six arms correctly from the answers. The\n    21\ttwo disagreements are judgement calls that do not move the verdict. **PASS** by both readers.\n    22\t\n    23\t## What it shows and what it does not\n    24\t- **What the link changed:** every linked v9 answer, every day, took Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue projection (e195, in v14's\n    25\t  conclusion) as its reference and translated it at the reported 92% mix, saying it is not a Data Center consensus; two of three sized\n    26\t  their forecast from v14's forecast. The controls had no market figure for the segment and built a run-rate from the Q2 total guide,\n    27\t  then added uplifts they did not size ($14B, $48B, an unstated second-half growth).\n    28\t- **The stored annual contradiction did not recur.** On 07-12 the stored run had Data Center $400–410B against a total of $387B. In all\n    29\t  three fresh controls the total rose to ~$411B on 07-12, so the annual Data Center figure stayed below it. What remains in the controls\n    30\t  is in the second half, which C as written cannot see (v14 states no second-half quarters): control-2 implies a Data Center second half\n    31\t  above the company's ($245.75B against $239.4B); control-3 leaves the rest of NVIDIA about $2.6B for the half against $6.35B in Q1.\n    32\t- **The verdict rests on B for the controls.** Read leniently (a disclosed but unsized uplift counts as supported), all three controls\n    33\t  reconcile and the result is inconclusive. Both readers failed control-2 and control-3 on B as written.\n    34\t- **Coherence is not accuracy:** the linked forecasts inherit v14's growth assumption and carry the Q1 mix forward.\n    35\t- **Scope:** one draw-window of three days, one pair, the market figure in hand for the total from the start. It says nothing yet about\n    36\t  other pairs, a whole run, or what links the matcher chooses elsewhere.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Added after the result (2026-11-21, while designing D699): the link's margins\n    39\tThe three linked sequences each asked Jev the 07-10 opening of v14 against v9 afresh (the same request, sha 6dcb7664…; answers in\n    40\teach sequence's archive, call 8738). \"v9 reads v14\" won with 0.52, 0.54 and 0.51; \"v14 reads v9\" had 0.47, 0.45 and 0.48. The rule\n    41\taccepts Jev's choice at 0.5 or more, so a shift of about 0.02 gives no link or the opposite link. The same opening's v14–v8 pair\n    42\t(stand-alone CPU) had \"v14 reads v8\" at 0.47, 0.46 and 0.44 (below the line, no link). \"Chosen in 3 of 3\" above is true, but the\n    43\tdirection was close to a coin flip; the whole-window design measures this class before buying any judgement\n    44\t(`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md` §3).\n     1\t# The July test: reading rule, frozen 2026-11-21 before any send (D694 (c))\n     2\t\n     3\tCopied from `TEST-PLAN.md` without change; scored line by line as written.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Reading rule (frozen before the send; council R round 2, `codex-r2.md` §7(b), with round 3)\n     6\t- **A. Comparable answers:** both 07-12 answers give a FY2027 forecast in USD for their own quantity (a range by its stated ends);\n     7\t  quarters recorded where both give comparable figures; a missing annual figure leaves reconciliation untested.\n     8\t- **B. Arithmetic and sources:** reported figures keep their amount and scope; the arithmetic follows from the stated inputs within\n     9\t  rounding; each assumption that materially sizes the result is disclosed and supported by the material available that day; a linked\n    10\t  forecast is treated as a forecast with assumptions.\n    11\t- **C. Room for the rest:** the high end of the Data Center forecast is at least $6B below the low end of the company forecast (the Q1\n    12\t  floor: $81.6B total minus $75.25B Data Center); each comparable quarter keeps the segment within the total.\n    13\t- **D. The link did the work (linked arm):** matching chose \"v9 reads v14\", the writer committed it at seq 148, and v9 used v14's\n    14\t  forecast in its calculation or as a stated bound; delivery and use of the Bloomberg-compiled $393B (e195) recorded separately; a\n    15\t  mention or citation alone earns no credit.\n    16\t- **Pass:** at least two of three linked sequences meet A–D; at most one control meets A–C; no first-attempt refusal added and no new\n    17\t  material grounding error on either question across 07-10..07-12 (relabelling a system forecast as a market estimate, or treating\n    18\t  $393B as a Data Center consensus, is one). **Inconclusive:** two or more controls reconcile, fewer than two comparable sequences in\n    19\t  either arm, or a ceiling stop. **Fail:** a new material error, an added refusal, fewer than two correct link selections, or any other\n    20\t  miss. Failures take precedence.\n    21\t- **Readers:** Claude's marks written first, then a fresh blind Codex reader on the six sequences under shuffled labels, the key outside\n    22\t  the repository; every sequence reported.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md | sed -n '1,115p'; rg -n '\"'^(##|'\"\\\\|)|D537' ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md | sed -n '1,115p'; rg -n '\"'^(##|'\"\\\\|)|D537' ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The July test: harness, archive, budget and reading rule (D694 (c); for council review before code)\n     2\t\n     3\t## Starting state ($0, done)\n     4\t`rebuild_control.py` → `rebuild-control.log`: old-2 replayed under poc-v118 from 07-01 to 07-09 with a transport that refuses every\n     5\tsend (`only_objects={'NVDA'}`, the comparison launcher's default): 160 items, 55 judgements, all from the archive; equal to old-2's\n     6\tfinished store up to seq 147 on views, keys, rungs, evidence, links, judgements (rung, reasons, cites, changed, reference, implied\n     7\toutcome, request sha), view switches, successions and dependencies. Each sequence starts from its own copy of this ledger.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Before any send ($0, to be built and run)\n    10\t1. **Prefix compatibility:** poc-v119 over 07-01..07-09 from empty, the link questions answered by a fixed `neither` transport, every\n    11\t   other request from the archive: the ledger equals the rebuilt prefix. This proves only that v119 with no link reproduces the\n    12\t   prefix; what a live v119 would have selected before 07-10 is not counted, and the linked arm starts from the v118 prefix by design\n    13\t   (the July test accepts the existing-reader / new-target case only, D694 (b)). The eligible pairs v119 would have asked before\n    14\t   07-10 are listed from the run's `link_selections` so the reader can see them.\n    15\t2. **The archive, proved:** each sequence's archive is a writable copy of old-2's archive (call ids kept) in which every stored judge\n    16\t   row for v9 or v14 (request user text beginning \"Thesis v9 (\" or \"Thesis v14 (\") is retired by the existing `Archive.retire`\n    17\t   (its request sha gains a suffix), so no exact lookup can return it. At $0 with a recording fake transport: an exact old v9 request\n    18\t   goes to the transport; an exact old request of another question is answered from the archive; an answer the check refuses is\n    19\t   retired and is not returned again.\n    20\t3. **Dry run:** the whole harness for 07-10..07-12 with a fixed fake transport for every call that would be bought: two control and\n    21\t   two linked dry sequences whose 07-10 answers for v9 and v14 differ (labelled \"DRY-A\", \"DRY-B\"). Every request that runs after the\n    22\t   first must carry its own sequence's last accepted answer. The control must judge v9 only on the days the stored run did (no v9\n    23\t   judgement on 07-11). The linked dry sequences must write \"v9 reads v14\" at seq 148 (fixed Jev answers) and show the judging-v8 block\n    24\t   in v9's requests; one with v14 `changed=true` on 07-11 must queue v9 that day, one with `changed=false` must not. Every other\n    25\t   request must be answered from the archive. Both stop paths are exercised with the recording fake: an unapproved request (a third\n    26\t   question's judge call) is refused before any reservation; an otherwise valid link request at another sequence than 148 is refused\n    27\t   before any reservation; a committed out-of-pair link whose reader would never be judged stops the sequence at inspection, before a\n    28\t   further reservation.\n    29\t4. **The rebuilt requests priced** at published rates (`model.PRICES`) to set the reservation; the reading rule frozen.\n    30\t\n    31\t## The sequences\n    32\tThree linked (poc-v119) and three control (poc-v118), each: its own copy of the prefix ledger, marked harness-only (DESIGN §0);\n    33\t`only_objects={'NVDA'}`; 07-10 to 07-12; `replay.run` with the harness's **serial item runner** as its `day_runner`: for each routed\n    34\titem, in order, it calls `round.run` with a transport bound to that item's own sequence number, then yields the result; the day's\n    35\tjudging gets the judging-scope transport through `replay.run`'s `transport`.\n    36\t\n    37\t**Archive per sequence:** its own writable copy of old-2's archive with the v9 and v14 judge rows retired (step 2 above), so both\n    38\tquestions are judged fresh every day (council R round 3) and every other request replays by exact identity. The harness holds\n    39\t`live_lock.acquire` on each sequence's store for the run, refuses a store whose last day is unfinished, and never resumes a stopped\n    40\tsequence (DESIGN §0).\n    41\t\n    42\t**The allow-list, in front of the budget transport:** every request that misses the archive reaches a wrapper before the budget\n    43\ttransport. The judging-scope wrapper passes only a judge request whose user text begins \"Thesis v9 (\" or \"Thesis v14 (\"; the item-scope\n    44\twrapper, built by the serial item runner for one item, passes only Jev requests whose questions are all link questions (the READS and\n    45\tSIGN builders' keys), and only when that item's sequence is 148, in the linked arm. Anything else raises before any reservation, which\n    46\tstops the sequence. The wrapper counts, per target judgement, the provider attempts; the harness asserts\n    47\tthat every v9 and v14 judgement of 07-10..07-12 was answered by a provider attempt of its own sequence (fresh), never by the archive.\n    48\t\n    49\t**The out-of-pair stop at inspection:** when `round.run` returns successfully for seq 148, the serial item runner reads the store's\n    50\t`dependencies` rows with `kind='reads'` before it starts the next item or yields to the day's judging: any row whose reader is not v9\n    51\tor v14 stops the sequence there (not comparable), whether or not that reader would ever be judged.\n    52\t\n    53\t**Transport:** the shared reservation ledger of the comparison runs (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py`\n    54\t`SharedLedger`, `BudgetTransport`, with `install_stop_gate`), one ceiling of **$0.15** for all six sequences, used as it behaves: a\n    55\tLuna call reserves its worst case per attempt and settles at its reported usage; a Jev call reserves its whole internal retry bundle\n    56\tand settles at up to the attempts times the reported usage, or at the reservation when usage is unavailable. After a sequence stops,\n    57\tthe shared ledger is read: a refused reservation makes the comparison **inconclusive**, even where `round.run` reports it as a matching\n    58\tor judging failure; an overrun or an unknown charge is reported from the ledger.\n    59\t\n    60\t**Expected new requests:** the v9 and v14 judge calls, the link questions of the item at seq 148 (linked arm), and a v9 judge call\n    61\ton any day it is queued by a changed v14. **Any other archive miss** (extraction, matching, another question's judgement) stops that\n    62\tsequence and is reported. Registered now for extra links the real selection may write besides \"v9 reads v14\" (for example with the\n    63\tstand-alone CPU question v8): the selection is kept and reported; a link whose reader is v9 or v14 changes a scored request and the\n    64\tsequence is scored as it ran, with the extra link named in the report; a link whose reader is any other question stops the sequence\n    65\tat inspection (above), and the sequence counts as not comparable. A missing \"v9\n    66\treads v14\", or v14 reading v9, is a selection miss.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Reading rule (frozen before the send; council R round 2, `codex-r2.md` §7(b), with round 3)\n    69\t- **A. Comparable answers:** both 07-12 answers give a FY2027 forecast in USD for their own quantity (a range by its stated ends);\n    70\t  quarters recorded where both give comparable figures; a missing annual figure leaves reconciliation untested.\n    71\t- **B. Arithmetic and sources:** reported figures keep their amount and scope; the arithmetic follows from the stated inputs within\n    72\t  rounding; each assumption that materially sizes the result is disclosed and supported by the material available that day; a linked\n    73\t  forecast is treated as a forecast with assumptions.\n    74\t- **C. Room for the rest:** the high end of the Data Center forecast is at least $6B below the low end of the company forecast (the Q1\n    75\t  floor: $81.6B total minus $75.25B Data Center); each comparable quarter keeps the segment within the total.\n    76\t- **D. The link did the work (linked arm):** matching chose \"v9 reads v14\", the writer committed it at seq 148, and v9 used v14's\n    77\t  forecast in its calculation or as a stated bound; delivery and use of the Bloomberg-compiled $393B (e195) recorded separately; a\n    78\t  mention or citation alone earns no credit.\n    79\t- **Pass:** at least two of three linked sequences meet A–D; at most one control meets A–C; no first-attempt refusal added and no new\n    80\t  material grounding error on either question across 07-10..07-12 (relabelling a system forecast as a market estimate, or treating\n    81\t  $393B as a Data Center consensus, is one). **Inconclusive:** two or more controls reconcile, fewer than two comparable sequences in\n    82\t  either arm, or a ceiling stop. **Fail:** a new material error, an added refusal, fewer than two correct link selections, or any other\n    83\t  miss. Failures take precedence.\n    84\t- **Readers:** Claude's marks written first, then a fresh blind Codex reader on the six sequences under shuffled labels, the key outside\n    85\t  the repository; every sequence reported.\n9:## 0. Versions and entry points\n33:## 1. Where selection runs: after matching has settled what opens\n69:## 2. The two questions\n87:matching step), D537 and council R (a part reads its whole), judging-v7 lines 4 and 9 (\"a part cannot exceed its whole\"; \"consistent\n96:## 3. What the reader is shown (judging-v8)\n111:## 4. The graph, the circle correction and the writer\n136:## 5. The label\n153:## 6. Table 1: state × event (C new in this article, T open and shown)\n158:| Event | Outcome |\n159:|---|---|\n160:| matching settles C as `new`/`succession`; the pair is eligible; READS and SIGN pass | link proposed; graph check; written in C's commit; reader judged in that day's round after the read question, if eligible (D59) |\n161:| READS `neither` / below 0.5; SIGN `no_clear_sign` / below 0.5 | no link; recorded |\n162:| C merges into an existing question, is skipped or not opened, or coalesces with another candidate | not asked, or asked once for the canonical candidate |\n163:| a pair not eligible (theme, event, two-rung, untyped or shuffled bounds, same article) | not asked (D695) |\n164:| a proposed link closes a circle (with stored links, or among the article's own proposals) | one correction request per link on it; a remaining circle fails the article at `matched`, resumable |\n165:| the store holds an unbound waiting row | the link step refuses: the article fails at `matched` with that reason |\n166:| `jev.ask` fails (transport, answer shape) | the article fails at `matched`, resumable; a resume re-asks what the archive lacks |\n167:| the plan no longer fits at write time (T closed in between, a circle) | `WriteConflict`; the article fails, resumable |\n168:| a crash after selection and before the write | resume re-runs matching and selection from the archive (no new send) and writes once |\n169:| a crash after the write | the link is in the article's commit; a resumed day's round rebuilds its queue (`queued_by_ledger`) |\n170:| T's conclusion changes in a round | its reader queued once, after it (`queued_by_change`) |\n171:| T's comparison point changes with `changed=true` | the reader is queued in the same round and sees it |\n172:| T's comparison point changes with `changed=false` | the reader sees it at its next judgement (§3) |\n173:| T closes | the reader keeps reading T's closing conclusion marked \"closed\" (PRD 06's numeric branch) |\n174:| the reader closes | not judged; the row stays as history |\n175:| a later article shows C and T again | not asked: neither is new (two open questions are a later version) |\n176:| an awaited (waiting) row in the store | this version writes none; the admission rule above refuses a store holding one |\n177:| a second process writing the same store | refused: the harness holds `live_lock.acquire` on each sequence's store for the run, and v119 has no other entry point (§0) |\n178:| a process dies after a provider attempt and before its answer reaches the archive | a death before settlement leaves the reservation counted against the ceiling (`SharedLedger.reserve`); a death after settlement leaves the settled charge counted; the sequence is stopped and final (§0), so no retry is free |\n179:| a version switch between completed days | allowed: the store's links and kinds are read the same by either version (§0); the July test switches from poc-v118 to poc-v119 at 07-10 |\n180:| a restart midway through a day (any configuration or input) | refused for v119: the harness refuses a store whose last day is unfinished and never resumes a stopped sequence (§0) |\n181:| a v118 run on a v119 store | §0 |\n183:## 7. Table 2: value × reader\n185:| Value | Readers |\n186:|---|---|\n187:| `dependencies` row with `kind='reads'` | judging: `topological`, `upstream`, `build_prompt`, `check_answer`, `queued_by_change`, `queued_by_ledger`; `store.would_cycle`, `dependants_of`, `dependencies_of`; `links.check_graph`; the service (`dependencies`, `dependants`), `bundle`, `search` records, `research_context` (explicit columns), the idea route: all read \"R depends on T\". `store.direct_parents` (filing) skips it. |\n188:| `kind=''` | every reader as today |\n189:| the `link_selections` row | the July test's readers and its shape test; no product reader, not preflight, not the trace |\n190:| T added to `changed` | `round.changed_views_of_day` → `judge_day` |\n191:| `current_state.reference`, `.implied_outcome` | the judging-v8 block only |\n193:## 8. Table 3: new path × old checks\n195:| Check | Under poc-v119 |\n196:|---|---|\n197:| no-link requests byte-equal | proved at $0: poc-v119 over old-2's 07-01..07-09 with the link questions answered by a fixed `neither` transport equals the rebuilt v118 prefix (every other request from the archive) |\n198:| filing under seed-style rows | unchanged (`kind ''`); the existing test stays green |\n199:| filing with a `reads` row | no link removed: re-file old-2's stored readings after seq 148 with the link present (105 shared statements, `filing-exposure.md`) |\n200:| `cites_views` only names shown dependencies | unchanged |\n201:| dependencies judged first | unchanged |\n202:| `matching.check` refusals fail the article | selection runs after it and never adds to its refusals |\n203:| preflight and the trace (read `match_records` by `call_no`) | untouched: the selection is in `link_selections` |\n204:| D59, the theme block | unchanged |\n205:| frozen-piece pins | §0 |\n207:## 9. Table 4: prior decisions\n216:## 10. Round-4 findings and where they are answered\n231:## 11. Tests (with the code; each names its row)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
